<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Communal Award</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1932">1932</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as MacDonald Award</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Communal Award was announced by the British Prime Minister Ramsay MacDonald on 16 August 1932, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/round-table-conferences-india">Round Table Conferences</a>, and extended separate electorates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> to the Depressed Classes, now known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheduled-castes">Scheduled Castes</a>, alongside Muslims, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a>, Indian Christians, Anglo-Indians and Europeans. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar">B. R. Ambedkar</a> supported separate electorates for the Scheduled Castes, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a>, who feared that the award would break up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> society, declared a fast unto death from Yerwada Jail in September 1932. After long negotiations Gandhi and Ambedkar agreed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poona-pact">Poona Pact</a> on a single Hindu electorate with seats reserved for the Scheduled Castes.</p></div>
